.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt CLOVED 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cloved
.Nd I2P-only BitTorrent daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl C
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a BitTorrent daemon that speaks only I2P.
It hosts the torrent engine, keeps state on disk, and serves a local HTTP API
for
.Xr clove 1 .
Peers are I2P destinations; there is no IP or port vocabulary anywhere in the
engine, and only the network layer may open a socket at all
.Pq see Sx SECURITY .
.Pp
.Nm
requires an external I2P router exposing SAMv3
.Pq i2pd or Java I2P .
It connects to that router in the background and retries on an exponential
backoff, so it may be started before the router is ready: torrents simply sit
in the
.Sy waiting-for-router
state until a session is established.
If the session is later lost, the whole session tree is torn down and rebuilt
on the same backoff, and torrents return to
.Sy waiting-for-router
in the meantime rather than failing.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl C , Fl -check
Parse and validate the configuration, print the resolved paths, and exit
without starting the daemon.
Use this before restarting a running daemon.
.It Fl c , Fl -config Ar file
Read configuration from
.Ar file
instead of the default path.
A file named here must exist; the default path may be absent, in which case
the built-in defaults are the entire configuration.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.El
.Sh RUNTIME
On start
.Nm
creates its data directory, generates an API token and a destination key
.Pq 32 random bytes, hex, mode 0600
if none exists, loads previously added torrents from disk, and binds the
control socket.
Torrents that were not paused are brought online as soon as a router session
exists; paused ones stay offline until
.Cm clove resume .
.Pp
Progress is snapshotted to each torrent's resume file every 30 seconds and
around every lifecycle change.
State files are written with a temporary file, an
.Xr fsync 2 ,
and a rename, so a crash at any point leaves the previous state intact; the
worst outcome is re-verification.
.Pp
.Nm
runs in the foreground and logs to standard error.
Under a service manager, run it as an ordinary unprivileged user: it needs no
elevated rights, only its own data directory and the router's SAM port.
.Sh SECURITY
clove is I2P-only by construction, not by configuration:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Only the
.Sy i2pnet
crate may open a socket; the engine cannot, and this is enforced by lint and
by a dependency gate in CI rather than by convention.
.It
The only outbound IP socket is the connection to the SAM bridge, which must be
a loopback address.
A remote
.Ic sam_address
is refused when the configuration is parsed; there is no override.
.It
There is no name resolution outside I2P: hostnames are resolved by SAM naming
only, never by DNS.
.It
Announce URLs in a torrent that are not I2P URLs are dropped at parse time.
They are never contacted, and never logged beyond a count.
.El
.Pp
The API token is required on every request, including over the unix socket.
Keep the data directory readable only by the daemon's user.
.Ss Self-restriction
Once initialisation is finished \(em configuration read, data directory
created, token loaded, torrents loaded, control socket bound \(em
.Nm
drops the capabilities it no longer needs, before it starts any thread.
On Linux this is
.Xr landlock 7 ,
confining the filesystem to the data directory, to the rights it needs rather
than to all of them, confining outbound TCP to the single SAM port, and, on
Landlock ABI 9 or later, refusing to connect to any
.Xr unix 7
socket; plus a
.Xr seccomp 2
allowlist: every syscall the daemon no longer needs returns
.Er ENOSYS ,
the answer a libc's fallback paths are written for.
Four calls that the daemon does still need are restricted by argument as well:
.Xr socket 2
to
.Dv AF_INET
and
.Dv SOCK_STREAM ,
the only combination it opens a socket with after this point \(em which is also
what keeps a UDP datagram from reaching anywhere Landlock's TCP rule does not
cover;
.Xr ioctl 2
to
.Dv FIONBIO ,
its only use here, closing off interface enumeration through
.Dv SIOCGIFCONF ;
and
.Xr mmap 2
and
.Xr mprotect 2
to mappings that are never writable and executable at once.
.Pp
An allowlist rather than a list of refusals, because a list of refusals can only
name what somebody thought of.
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr ptrace 2 ,
module and BPF loading, mount and namespace calls, kernel replacement, the
kernel keyring and
.Xr io_uring_setup 2
are all denied \(em not because each is named, but because none is allowed.
The permitted set is measured from a traced run of the daemon rather than
guessed at, and the daemon's own test suite performs that workload under the
live filter.
.Pp
Neither mechanism is allowed to fail startup.
What was actually applied is reported in one line on standard error, for
example
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cloved: sandbox: landlock enforced (unix-socket connects denied too); seccomp filter installed
.Ed
.Pp
On a kernel below Landlock ABI 9 the same line says that unix-socket connects
are unrestricted.
When Landlock cannot be applied at all, the kernel is asked why rather than
assumed at, and the line names one of three causes: Landlock absent from the
build entirely, present but not enabled in the
.Va lsm=
list, or an ABI below the 6 that
.Nm
needs \(em in which case it reports the version it found rather than the one it
wanted.
Either way the daemon continues, unless
.Ic sandbox Ar require
is set in
.Xr clove.conf 5 ,
which makes an incomplete confinement a refusal to start rather than a line in
the log.
It is off by default, so this is one of three independent layers and no layer
assumes another is present.
.Pp
What was achieved is reported on the startup line and, for the life of the
process, in
.Sy sandbox
from
.Xr clove 1 Cm status
and
.Pa /v1/status .
That matters because the failure mode of a best-effort layer is silence: a
daemon on which neither mechanism applied behaves exactly like one on which
both did.
The others are the by-construction restrictions above and the OS sandbox in
.Pa contrib/systemd ,
which ships a system unit and a user unit:
only the system one can apply the
.Va IPAddressDeny=
clearnet lock, which systemd offers to system services alone.
.Nm make Cm install
picks between them by prefix.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Pa ~/.config/clove/clove.conf
Default configuration file; see
.Xr clove.conf 5 .
.It Pa ~/.local/share/clove/
Data directory.
.It Pa ~/.local/share/clove/token
API token, mode 0600.
.It Pa ~/.local/share/clove/destination.key
The client's I2P destination keys, mode 0600.
Written after the router first accepts a session and reused on every start, so
the destination is stable across restarts.
Absent when
.Ic ephemeral
is set; see
.Xr clove.conf 5 .
Anyone holding this file can be you on the network.
.It Pa ~/.local/share/clove/lock
Instance lock, held with
.Xr flock 2
for the life of the process.
A second
.Nm
started on the same data directory refuses to start rather than take over
the socket and write into the same state files; the lock is released by the
kernel when the holder exits, however it exits, so nothing stale is left
behind.
.It Pa ~/.local/share/clove/state/
Per-torrent state: the original
.Pa .torrent ,
resume data, and pending magnet URIs.
.It Pa ~/.local/share/clove/downloads/
Downloaded files.
.It Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/clove.sock
Default control socket.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check the configuration, then run:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cloved -C
cloved: configuration OK
  config     /home/u/.config/clove/clove.conf (absent; using defaults)
  data_dir   /home/u/.local/share/clove
  api_socket /run/user/1000/clove.sock
  sam_address 127.0.0.1:7656
$ cloved
cloved: 3 torrent(s) loaded
cloved: listening on /run/user/1000/clove.sock
cloved: router connected; we are asubu6d7wsbj7x7b7agamhimpmnlr3ezb7qu5zh2jllyalblxdpq.b32.i2p
.Ed
.Pp
Run under a user service manager:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ systemctl --user start clove
$ journalctl --user -u clove -f
.Ed
.Pp
Start before the router is up.
The daemon waits rather than exiting:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cloved
cloved: waiting for router (SAM at 127.0.0.1:7656): Connection refused
$ clove status
version      2026.08
router       waiting-for-router
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr clove 1 ,
.Xr clove.conf 5 ,
.Xr clove-api 7
.Sh STANDARDS
BEP 3 (peer protocol), BEP 6 (fast extension), BEP 9 (metadata exchange),
BEP 10 (extension protocol), BEP 12 (multiple trackers), BEP 20 (peer id),
and the I2P BitTorrent dialect: 32-byte destination hashes in compact tracker
responses and
.Sy i2p_pex .
clove's reading of the underspecified corners is recorded in the
.Pa PROTOCOL.i2p-bt
file shipped with the source.
.Sh CAVEATS
i2p_dht, UDP tracker announces, and BitTorrent v2 are not implemented.
Peer discovery is by tracker announce and peer exchange.
